# function sum(list, rounds) { let total = 0; if (list !== undefined) { for (let i = 0; i < rounds; i++) total += list.length; } return total; }   with `undefined | string | number[]` expected of `list`
# The body asks a test of the type of the parameter, and that test passes every member of the union or none: behind it `list.length` is as little typed as in front. A guard that let all of the union through would have nothing
# that leans on it, and there was none: every copy of such a helper lost its typed body and built its loops twice (the bundle of the TypeScript compiler: 2,226 loops more, GenerateCode of a test run +4.2%).
# It keeps the guard for the member that one test tells. undefined, which the body itself sends another way, passes too: such a helper is handed undefined as often as a list, and a guard for the list alone
# sent every such call into the slow version. null, for which the body does not ask, does not pass: it would stand beside the list in all of the loop, and the loop would be built twice.
# f2 asks for a number, which no member is: the same.
# f3 tells the members apart and leans on neither arm, and reads the length in front of the test in a loop: with all of the union let through too little leans, and the guard for the list still pays.
# RUN: plan units mechanisms=all
# CHECK: unit f1:ParameterGuard {{*}} tests (undefined|Array<number>, number)
# CHECK: unit f2:ParameterGuard {{*}} tests (Array<number>, number)
# CHECK: unit f3:ParameterGuard {{*}} tests (Array<number>, number)
# RUN(WALK): walk f1:Body mechanisms=all
# WALK: ([[list]]: undefined|Array<number>{{*}}entry=0
# WALK: = GetArrayLength
# RUN(CLOSED): walk f1:Body mechanisms=all
# CLOSED-NOT: PropGet
# RUN(ONCE): walk f1:Body mechanisms=all
# ONCE: LoopHint
# ONCE-NOT: LoopHint

module m0 path="main.ts" displayPath="main.ts" body=f0
entry module=m0
